{
  "schemaVersion": "1.11",
  "kind": "archsim.architecture",
  "metadata": {
    "name": "Marketplace search on OpenSearch",
    "description": "Product search on a marketplace. Shoppers on the web shop and the mobile app search (95% of requests); sellers edit their listings from the same apps (5%). An ALB sends searches to a Go search API, which looks the query up in a Redis query cache (cache-aside; about 40% of searches repeat a recent one) and otherwise searches OpenSearch: 3 primary shards of 30 GiB with one replica each, on six 8-vCPU nodes. Every search runs on one copy of each shard and waits for the slowest, plus the coordinating node's merge. Edits go to a Listings API, which publishes them to a Kafka topic keyed by listing; a Java indexer writes them to OpenSearch, where an index write waits until the replica has indexed the document too. 1,400 req/s. The scenarios try three classic OpenSearch questions: how many shards, how many replicas, and what a slow cluster does behind a short timeout. All numbers are illustrative assumptions.",
    "tags": [
      "search",
      "opensearch",
      "sharding",
      "replicas",
      "kafka",
      "redis",
      "cache-aside",
      "client-groups"
    ]
  },
